# Maps each NetApp SVM (vserver) to the ONTAP REST API base URL to
# reach it on - the same data LIF already used for that SVM's
# NFS/CIFS backup traffic (management service enabled on it), not a
# separate cluster management LIF. One line per SVM, not per share:
# several shares/volumes commonly live on the same SVM and share one
# line here.
#
# "<SVM name>  <https://host-or-ip-of-that-SVM's-LIF>"
#
# The user/password used against every SVM's API is the same
# everywhere (NETAPP_API_USER/NETAPP_API_PASSWORD in secrets.env) -
# only the endpoint differs, which is what this file is for. If a
# given cluster instead uses a per-SVM password, this project doesn't
# support that yet - ask for it if you need it.
#
# IMPORTANT: use the SVM's hostname here, not its LIF's raw IP. ONTAP's
# self-signed certificate on a data LIF is issued with the SVM name as
# its subject/SAN (confirmed on a real cluster: `curl ... https://<ip>`
# fails with "SSL: certificate subject name 'SVMCIGOUVZ1001' does not
# match target host name '<ip>'") - and this project never disables
# TLS verification to work around that. Instead, add a line to
# /etc/hosts (or an internal DNS record) resolving that exact name to
# the LIF's IP, e.g.:
#   10.110.136.43   SVMCIGOUVZ1001
# then point this file at the name, as below. Use
# `openssl s_client -connect <ip>:443 -showcerts </dev/null 2>/dev/null
# | openssl x509 -noout -subject -ext subjectAltName` to confirm the
# exact name a given LIF's certificate expects.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored. Copy to
# /etc/restic/netapp-svms.conf.

svm-cs3-bnl-dev     https://svm-cs3-bnl-dev
SVMCIGOUVZ1001      https://SVMCIGOUVZ1001
